                                    3.    Closed sets and numerical certificates


   The numerical compatibility calculation keeps the two original dipole balls closed. Finite bi-
nary64 coordinates and radii are treated as exact rational inputs for squared-ball membership and
emptiness. Zero-radius balls and external tangency are handled before optimization. A rounded
tangent point is accepted only if it satisfies both original predicates. Failure to represent or verify
a feasible witness returns numerical uncertainty, not physical exclusion or an enlarged radius.
   An optimizer supplies a candidate, not a compatibility certificate. A verified feasible candi-
date supplies a primal residual bound. A lower bound follows from the seven-component support
                                                                                                    18


inequality: for ∥u∥ ≤ 1,



                               r∗ ≥ uT O
                                       b − hD (KST u),     KS = κLSb,                           (A10)

   where the support function obeys



                              hD (v) ≤ min{v T dobs + rd ∥v∥, Dmax ∥v∥}.                        (A11)

   If the amplitude ball is omitted, only the first expression is used. This support bound need not
be sharp. The implementation uses candidate residual and observation directions and incorporates
its declared response-roundoff envelopes. It exports a lower endpoint rounded downward and an
upper endpoint rounded upward. The positive-budget score is the upward-rounded quotient of the
exported upper endpoint and B; it is an upper bound, not a claim to have computed the exact
minimizing score.
   For a general nondegenerate case, the implemented decisions compare the upper endpoint be-
fore display rounding with B + δloc , or the lower endpoint with B + δglob . With binary64 machine
